1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Folketelling 1885 for 0301 Kristiania kjøpstad'''
2 CONT https://www.digitalarkivet.no/census/person/pf01053257053327
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''vielsen:'''
2 CONT https://www.digitalarkivet.no/view/327/pv00000004833358
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathilde was the first daughter and second child of shoemaker/emissary Carsten Lutken Woll and Matilde Helene Krum Woll. She lived in Bergen, Norway as a young child, then her family moved to Kristiania in 1860s and later Fredriks
2 CONC tad, Kongsberg, and Fredrikshald due to her father's missionary work.
2 CONT
2 CONT At age 24 she married Ole Kristian Pedersen on January 6, 1880 in Kristiania. They had three children: sons Erling (b. Feb.1881), Thoralf (b. March 1883) and daughter Alfhild (b. May 4,1885). Continuing her family's obvious love f
2 CONC or music, Mathilde taught piano while living in Kristiania. Husband Ole died of erysipelas in January of 1885 in Kristiania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathilde boarded the vessel Geiser and emigrated to the USA with her three small children in May, 1886. She eventually
2 CONT settled in the Green Lake area of Seattle, Washington, and became owner of the Green Lake Grocery and on December 5, 1894 she was appointed as postmaster. Mathilde's brother Jacob Andreas Woll and cousin Ray Martin(Ragnvald) Krum 
2 CONC both boarded at Mathilde's residence for a time. Jacob and Mathilde jointly operated the grocery in Green Lake in the early 1890s. Mathilde eventually took over ownership of the store until her death in 1907. Mathilde was affectio
2 CONC nately known as "Tilla" in her family, and was godmother to several of her siblings' children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathilde died at age 50 in Seattle on May 18, 1907 of uterine cancer.
2 CONT She is buried with her son Thorolf at beautiful Lake View Cemetery on Capitol Hill in Seattle.
