1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Utvandret 1881
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sites.google.com/site/totenemigrant/Toten-emigrant-1846/1881
2 CONT
2 CONT Gårdbruker (farmer). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Even Hansen Dyste was baptised on the 4th of september 1853.
2 CONT
2 CONT Faddere (godparents):
2 CONT
2 CONT Niels Christiansen Dyste 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peder Olsen Dyste
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders Guldbrandsen Dyste
2 CONT
2 CONT Berthe Maria Larsdatter Dyste
2 CONT
2 CONT Marthe Christiansdatter Dyste
2 CONT
2 CONT Even H. Dyste and his wife, Karoline, emigrated to America in 1881, the same year they got married. They traveled with Evens 19 year old brother, Johan. They left Kristiania (the name of Oslo at the time, often also called Christi
2 CONC ania) the 28th of april, with 100 kroner in their purse. The line was Chr. Sotaan and the boat was named Rollo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first 4 or 5 years they lived in Minneapolis, where also his brother Niels lived. In 1885 they decided to move to Forman, North Dakota - the lot.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Johan wrote: "I came to Forman in 1886 with my brothers, Even og Nils. The same years many other norwegians also arrived; among others Helmer Gaardløs, Peter Narum og Johan P. Lundstad. Everybody settled in a nabourhood sout
2 CONC h of Forman, with the acception of my brother Nils and me. We opened a store in Forman City and run it for 24 years. Nils later sold his share to me." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Even did visit Norway in 1907. He travelled to New York once more on the 23th of mars 1907 bringing along 2000 kroner. The line was M. R. Raffel and the ship was Montebello.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1910 United States Federal Census about Even H. Dyste: Home in 1910; Rutland, Sargent, North Dakota.
2 CONT
2 CONT Year of Immigration: 1881.
2 CONT
2 CONT Household Members: 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Even H. Dyste, 56.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karoline Dyste, 	55.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Dyste, 20.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma C. Dyste, 17.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clara P. Narum 	12.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rutland Leader, September 16, 1910:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Mrs. E. H. Dyste died at her home in Rutland township, Tuesday evening at 8:30 p. m. after a brief illness with typhoid fever. Mrs.Dyste had been nursing her twelve year old daughter with untiring effort until a week ago Thursday
2 CONC , when she was taken down with the dreaded disease to which she succumbed. Mrs. Dyste was born in Toten, Norway Oct. 13, 1854, and was wedded to her present husband in 1881. They came to this country April of the same year, and to
2 CONC ok up their residence in Minneapolis where they remained for five years. They were blessed with six children, three sons and three daughters. They arrived here in 1886 to make their permanent home and have led a prosperous and hap
2 CONC py life ever since. Mrs. Dyste leaves three brothers and one sister, a husband, and seven children to mourn
2 CONT
2 CONT her sudden demise. The death was a particularly sad one owning to her devotion to her daughter, Clara, who has been dangerously ill with the same disease. Funeral services were held at the new Lutheran church in Rutland township, 
2 CONC Thursday. The family has the sympathy of the entire community in their bereavement. Card of thanks: We wish to express our thanks and gratitude to the kind neighbors and friends who assisted us in the recent bereavement of a lovin
2 CONC g wife and mother. E. H. Dyste and family.”
2 CONT
2 CONT I 1914 var han også i Norge på besøk. Han reiste tilbake til Minnesota den 24. august 1914. Linjen var Jacobsen via Bergen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Even Dyste accompanied his brother, Nils, to Milnor in April of 1886. They walked to Forman, stopping enroute at the home of Nils Arnizen in Dunbar. Even decided to take a homestead southeast of Forman in Rutland Twp. so he sent f
2 CONC or his wife and three small children, Marie (later married Sannes) Laura (later married Jensen) and Hans. Children born in Dakota were John, Emma (later married Henjum) and Christ. The first summer the family lived in a sod shanty
2 CONC , but they soon built a two story frame house hauling the lumber from Milnor. His stock was housed in a sod barn, for several years until the neighbors horse walked on it and fell through, making it necessary to rebuild the barn. 
2 CONC The neighbors always knew when Even was working in his fields because his "Hi" or "Ho" to the oxen could be heard for miles around. Even was a charter member of Trinity Lutheran Church. His wife (Karolin Dyste) died in 1910 at whi
2 CONC ch time he sold his farm to his son Christ. In 1914 he visited his old home in Norway. On his return to the United States he married Marie Sibelrud and lived in Hartland, Minn. until her death in 1932. He came back to Forman and l
2 CONC ived with Christ until he became ill; then he made his home with his daughter, Laura (Mrs. Christ Jensen) in Breckenridge. He died in 1936. His granddaughter, Dorthea Nelson, and several grandchildren live in Forman and vicinity.
2 CONT
