1 NAME Johan Hansen /Dyste/
2 GIVN Johan Hansen
2 _MARNM Dyste
2 NICK John
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 31 JUL 1863
2 PLAC Dyste
2 ADDR
3 STAE Oppland
3 CTRY Norge
1 BAPM
2 DATE 23 AUG 1863
1 OCCU Storekeeper/Owner
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Utvandret 1881
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sites.google.com/site/totenemigrant/Toten-emigrant-1846/1881
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan Hansen Dyste was baptised on the 23th of  august 1863.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godparents: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Niels Christiansen Dyste 
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Guldbrandsen Dyste 
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Hansen Dyste
2 CONT
2 CONT Karen Simensdatter Dyste
2 CONT
2 CONT Karine Hansdatter Dyste
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan Dyste was the son of Hans og Berthe Marie Dyste, Kolbu.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan H. Dyste emigrated to America with his older brother, Even H. Dyste and his brothers wife, Karoline, in 1881. They left Kristiania (Oslo) on the 28th of april, with 100 kroner in their purse. The line was Chr. Sotaan and the
2 CONC  boat was called Rollo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan changed his name to John when he arrived at Ellis Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT He worked in Eden (now Hudson) S. D., in a store owned by Overseth and Dyste. The Dyste was his oldest brother, Peder (Peter). After a year for which he earned the price of a suit, he went to Minneapolis, Minn. to join his brother
2 CONC s Nels and Even, who were working i the market on Washington Ave. In 1885 Nels started a store in Forman (N.D.) and John started one in Rutland (N.D.) - only 24 years old. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nels H. and Hannah (Narum) Dyste, were natives of Norway but came to the United States in early life, settling in Minnesota. Their marriage was celebrated in Minneapolis and on removing to North Dakota Mr. Dyste took up land in Sa
2 CONC rgent county. Soon afterward, however, he turned his attention to merchandising and opened a general store, leaving brought a stock with him. Six years later his brother, John Dyste, entered into partnership with him and the busin
2 CONC ess relation between them was continued until 1909, when Nels H. Dyste retired and is now spending his days in the enjoyment of a well earned rest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The John Dystes moved from Rutland to Forman when it became necessary to consolidate the stocks of the two stores on account of poor crops and hard times. They lived in what became known as the O. B. Johnson house. It stood where 
2 CONC Russ Hosford now has a new house. In 1890 their first child, Bertha Marie, was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1924 he sold the store to his son and his bothers son.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1888 John visited his home in Norway and brought Mina Hoel back. They were married April 13, 1889 in Nels Dyste's home. John told about fighting a prairie fire that day and came to his wedding with his mustache burned off. The 
2 CONC fire threatened the town of Forman coming as close to the store as the lot where John Lee has his drug store
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan was active in the political life of Forman. He got one son and four daughters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan Dystes egne ord:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Når man kommer med Great Northern-banen til Belle Plaine eller Soo-banen til det som nå er Forman, befinner man seg i hva av mange blir kalt Nye Toten. Men er det noe her som minner om Toten? Ikke noe annet enn den dype, fruktbar
2 CONC e muldjorden med en overflødighet av kampestein. Her er ingen rislende bekker, brusende elver eller sukkende skoger av nål- og løvtrær, som lokket disse arbeidsvante, villige unge menn og kvinner som slo hjerte i livet og fortsatt
2 CONC e fra Minneapolis vestover for å søke at nytt hjem for seg og sine.
2 CONT
2 CONT I 1885 kom jeg og mine brødre, Even og Nils, hit til Forman. Samme år kom Helmer Gaardløs, Peter Narum og Johan P. Lundstad hit. Alle bosatte de seg i et nabolag syd for Forman, untagen min bror Nils og jeg, som startet opp en for
2 CONC retning i selve Forman og drev den i 24 år. Nils solgte sin andel til meg, og jeg fortsatte som innehaver inntil jeg tok inn min sønn, Harald, syv år senere. I 1924 solgte jeg min andel til Helge Dyste, som kom til Amerika i 1913 
2 CONC og nå driver forretningen sammen med Harald. 
2 CONT
2 CONT De ovennevnte familier dannet rygraden av den bekjente totenkolonien. Mange menn og kvinner strømmet inn her år etter år , for senere å fortsette til andre trakter av landet. Særlig i tiden fra 1885, såsnart de beskjedne boliger v
2 CONC ar oppført, pleides den medfødte norske selskapelighet mellom disse familier fra andre bygder slik at de ble regnet som hjemmehørende i Toten. Ved de årlige høytider og andre anledninger var det vanlig at man tok runden og besøkte
2 CONC  de alle. Kjære minner lever i erindringen fra den tiden, og især vi gamle minnes med vemod all den glede og hygge vi opplevde. John H. Dyste."
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilder:
2 CONT
2 CONT -1865-telling for 0529 Vestre Toten, Digitalarkivet. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -Totn, tidsskrift for Toten Historielag, hefte 4, (1959)
2 CONT
2 CONT side 309-310 (John E. Dyste brev fra Forman, North Dakota).
2 CONT
2 CONT -Emigranter over Kristiania 1871-1930, Digitalarkivet. 
2 CONT
